    `macunpack/dd.h` beside it, is the `DDAR` `0x20` raw-fork shape,
    which no specimen here reaches — it is also what names which forks
    use method 10's second form.
    And **the StuffIt 5 container** since 2026-09-22:
    `reference/notes/sit5_specs_theunarchiver.md` is that same wiki's
    `StuffIt5Format` page, which this project's own
    `reference/notes/StuffIt.md` had named in its *Sources* list all
    along, and `docs/formats/stuffit5-container.md` carries the one
    correction it needs — its archive-header table gives offset 84
    twice. A reader written from the page alone reads all 31 StuffIt 5
    fixtures, 525 entries, agreeing with the oracle on every path and
    both fork lengths (`tools/derive_sit5.py`). What is still
    XADStuffIt5Parser.m's in `src/fmt_sit5.c` is the ENCRYPTED archive
    — where the password hash sits and the MD5-of-MD5 that grades a
    password — which that page does not describe and which no corpus
    archive exercises.
    **Now Compress is a different case.** The FORMAT is described by
    this project — `docs/formats/now-compress.md`, measured off Now
    Compress 5.0.1 under emulation, with a reader written from that
    note alone decoding every block of both corpus archives
    (`tools/derive_now.py`) — because no published description of it
    exists anywhere. Its coders in `src/fmt_now.c` were **rewritten
    from that note on 2026-09-21**, the M12 transcription of
    XADNowCompressParser.m and XADNowCompressHandle.m having stood
    until then. **Nothing in that file rests on the reference any
    more**: the last two things that did — the offset-delta search and
    the code-length escape past 15, neither of which the encoder will
    produce on request — were measured against the format's own decoder
    the same day, by changing the field and handing the file back to
    Now Compress (`tools/mutate_now.py`).
    Files that transcribe XADMaster code carry a
    "Source: reference/XADMaster/<file>" comment.

  * macutils 2.0b3, by Dik T. Winter et al. (descended from Allan G.
    Weber's unsit and D. J. Perry's unstuffit) — late-80s C. Its README
    states that every algorithm in it is implemented from publicly
    available software, that use and redistribution are explicitly
    allowed, and that "for some parts of it the GNU software license
    does apply"; the files here that cite it say "LGPL, see NOTICE",
    and this is what that points at.
    Used as a secondary reference for the oldest formats. **Not
    `src/fmt_sit.c` since 2026-09-22**: that file's method 6, "fixed
    Huffman", was transcribed from `macunpack/sit.c` because XADMaster
    recognizes the method and refuses it, and it is
    `docs/formats/stuffit-method-6.md`'s now — deark implements the
    method under MIT, which the claim that macutils was the only
    reference for it had missed for four weeks (BLOCKERS.md).
    **Not `src/fmt_packit.c`'s container since 2026-09-21**: PackIt is
    published too, in appendices B and C of R. Scott McGinnis's 1986
    unPACKIT manual, and `docs/formats/packit.md` carries the four
    corrections they need — a reader written from those appendices
    alone reads all 38 forks of the corpus's PackIt archives
    (`tools/derive_packit.py`). What that file still owed macutils was
    that the two CRCs the appendix names are CRC-16/XMODEM, and the
    resync for an entry it cannot decode; the two ciphers were
    XADMaster's. **Neither since 2026-10-05**: `Packit III 1.3` ships
    inside the corpus fixture `packit.pit_.bin`, which makes the
    generation that wrote every `PMa5` and `PMa6` block the reference for
    both — witnessed at 32 offsets in its `CODE` 1 and then EXECUTED
    under `qemu-m68k` (`tools/derive_pitcrypt.py`). That file owes
    neither reference anything now.
    **And not hexbin.c's second-document scan since 2026-10-06**:
    DeHQX 2.0.1 documents it (below), and DeHQX is Peter Lewis's, whose
    BinHex 4.0 Definition that file is transcribed from.
    **And not `src/fmt_uu.c` since 2026-10-06**: the MU layout is
    UUTool's own, stated in UUTool's own manual and written out of
    literals its `DATA` 0 holds whole, and `mu_convert()`'s transform
    is mpack's `uudecodeline()` (below) with UUTool's `HEXA` 128 table
    for the pad character — all of it checked by `tools/derive_mu.py`
    against three versions of the application and two wild specimens.
    The claim that only hexbin read the layout had stood since M13
    while the application itself sat in the corpus as
    `fixtures/uu-tool-24.hqx` (BLOCKERS.md).
    A build of `macunpack` is also the second oracle for the
    differential test suite (TESTING.md) — test-time only, not linked
    into anything shipped.

  * LZHUF, by Haruyasu Yoshizaki (1988) with comments by Haruhiko
    Okumura (1989) — the published program, LZHUF.C, as distributed in
    http://cd.textfiles.com/rbbsv3n1/pac4/okumura.zip. It carries no
    copyright notice and no licence text; its terms of use are
    reconstructed (Russell Marks, 2001, via deark's readme-lzhuf.txt)
    as "use, distribute, and modify this program freely". Since
    2026-09-19 `src/unc_lzah.c` — the -lh1- coder shared by StuffIt
    method 5, LZH -lh1- and ARC 7.x Trimmed (and DART's "best" until
    that format left the engine on 2026-09-25) — follows
    that program's tree machinery (freq/son/prnt, reconst, update,
    the 256-entry distance lookup derived from the 64 code lengths)
    rather than XADMaster's transcription of it; the modes and
    everything around the tree are this project's. **The window
    preload for -lh1- and StuffIt method 5 is not**, and this notice
    said it was until 2026-09-26: that pattern — 13 copies of each
    byte, then 0..255, then 255..0, then zeros, then spaces — is
    XADLZHDynamicHandle.m's `resetLZSSHandle`. LHa for UNIX states the
    same five steps in `larc.c`'s `decode_start_lz5`, but for LArc's
    -lz5- and not for -lh1-, and lhasa fills the -lh1- buffer with
    spaces instead (`lh1_decoder.c`), so neither is a second statement
    of what this file does. The
    change was made for speed and heap, not licence: this engine
    remains LGPL through its other XADMaster transcriptions.

  * deark, by Jason Summers — https://github.com/jsummers/deark, MIT.
    Two ARC methods in `src/fmt_arc.c` follow deark rather than
    XADMaster: 0x0b ("Distilled"), whose reading comes from deark's
    `src/fmtutil-lzh.c`, and 0x0a as ARC 7.x means it ("Trimmed"),
    whose parameters — a stop code, match lengths 3..59 and a
    zero-filled window — come from `foreign/lzhuf.h`'s
    `is_arc_trimmed` branch, with the dialect test that tells Trimmed
    from PAK's Crushed taken from `modules/arc.c`. The first is
    followed instead of XADMaster's because XADMaster's is wrong
    about one thing and the CRC PAK stored says so: the 8 KB window
    starts full of spaces (BLOCKERS.md). No code was copied — the file
    states the facts it took and cites them — and deark's own
    documentation of the format (entropymine.wordpress.com,
    "ARC/PAK's Distilled compression scheme") is cited alongside it.
    Since 2026-09-19 deark is also the source of `src/unc_lzw.c`, the
    Unix compress(1) LZW decoder: `src/fmtutil-lzw.c` documents the
    stream — the clear code, where the code width grows, and the rule
    that codes are written eight at a time at one width, so a width
    change mid-group leaves padding to discard. The algorithm is
    Welch's. Again no code was copied, and `LZW.c` and
    `XADCompressHandle.m` are no longer sources of that file. The
    same file's `delzw_process_unixcompress_3byteheader` is what
    `src/fmt_exo.c` cites for the `.Z` header the decoder sits behind
    — the magic, the maximum code width in the mode byte's low five
    bits, and bit 7 for the clear code.
    **And since 2026-10-08 the same file's `DE_LZWFMT_ZIPSHRINK` paths
    are the source of zip method 1, Shrink, in `src/unc_shrink.c`
    (`src/unc_lzw.c` until 2026-10-11, moved unchanged)** — the
    escape code and its two commands in `delzw_process_code`,
    `delzw_partial_clear`, `delzw_find_first_free_entry`, and the two
    cases `delzw_process_data_code` and `delzw_emit_code` handle for a
    code whose slot is free. No code was copied. XADMaster's
    `XADZipShrinkHandle.m` is NOT a source: it performs the partial
    clear as a full one, and `unar` fails every entry that has one.
    **And since 2026-10-11 `foreign/ozunreduce.h` (Jason Summers,
    2019, written from APPNOTE.TXT, and offered under MIT OR as public
    domain, at the user's choice) is the implementation zip methods 2
    to 5, Reduce, in `src/unc_reduce.c` were read beside.** The rules
    are APPNOTE.TXT 5.2's — the follower sets read from 255 down to 0,
    B(N), the second stage's states around the escape byte 144, and the
    zeros before the start of the output — and where `ozur` and 5.2
    differ, on a copy longer than its distance, the file follows 5.2.
    No code was copied; the file's terms would permit it either way.
    XADMaster names the four methods (`XADZipParser.m`) and has no
    decoder for them, so `unar` refuses every such entry.
    `tools/synth_reduce.py`, the encoder behind a fixture and a fuzz
    seed, is written from 5.2 alone.
    **And since 2026-10-07 `reference/deark/src/fmtutil-lzh.c` is the
    reference implementation `src/fmt_exo.c`'s Implode decoder (zip
    method 6) was read beside** — `implode_read_a_tree`,
    `implode_read_trees` and `decompress_implode_internal`. That file
    is MIT like the rest of deark's `src/`, and outside the `foreign/`
    tree whose own licence terms the row above is about. No code was
    copied: every rule is in APPNOTE.TXT 5.3, which the file cites
    section by section at the fields, and the reference is what each
    reading was checked against — including the one thing the
    specification does not spell out, that a tree must be fully
    populated, which deark's own comment states in prose.
    **Its fourth parameter is implemented since 2026-10-08**: PKZIP
